/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/*--------------------------- File numeric.dox -----------------------------*/
/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/** \addtogroup area_numeric */
/** \defgroup theory_numeric Theory of numerical algorithms
 \ingroup area_numeric

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/numeric` realize the arithmetic of
 section 2.6 and of section 12.3 of the book (the integers of \f$ n \f$ bits
 and the polynomials, multiplied by divide et impera), the product of
 matrices of section 12.4, the recurrences of chapter 13 (the numbers of
 Fibonacci, the binomial coefficients, the chain of matrices) and the numbers
 of Catalan of the exercises on dynamic programming,
 the number theory of chapter 17 (Euclid, the modular powers, the test of
 Miller and Rabin, RSA), and, beyond the book, the integers and the
 fractions of any length, the fast Fourier transform, the generators of
 random numbers with their tests and the samplings, and the sparse matrices.
 This page collects what the headers take for granted: the notation shared by
 all of them (see \ref theory_numeric_notation), the representations of the
 numbers and of the matrices with the costs of their operations (see
 \ref theory_numeric_repr), and, for each family of algorithms, the property
 on which its correctness rests and the argument behind its bound (from
 \ref theory_numeric_dc to \ref theory_numeric_sparse), and the backends of
 GMP and of a CBLAS on which some of them run (see
 \ref theory_numeric_backends). The pseudocode of
 each algorithm is instead on the page of its header, in the comment of its
 function, together with the section of the book, or the paper, that it
 follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_numeric_notation Definitions and notation

 An integer is written in a base \f$ \beta \f$ as the digits
 \f$ x_0 , x_1 , \ldots , x_{n-1} \in [ 0 , \beta ) \f$, the least
 significant first, i.e., \f$ x = \sum_{i<n} x_i \beta^i \f$; the base is 2
 for the vectors of bits of `BinaryInteger.h` and \f$ 2^{64} \f$ for the
 digits (the <em>limbs</em>) of `BigInteger.h`, and the length \f$ n \f$ of
 the operands is the measure of the costs. A polynomial is the vector of its
 coefficients from the constant one, so that the product of two integers is
 the product of their polynomials in \f$ x = \beta \f$ followed by the
 propagation of the carries. A matrix is \f$ m \times n \f$, and a sparse one
 has \f$ \mathit{nnz} \f$ nonzero entries, the others being 0 and not
 stored.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ n \f$, \f$ \beta \f$</td>
     <td>the number of digits (bits, coefficients, rows) of an operand, and
         the base of the digits</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ M( n ) \f$</td>
     <td>the time of the product of two integers of \f$ n \f$ digits, in
         which the quotients, the roots, the conversions and the gcds are
         measured</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \gcd( a , b ) \f$, \f$ a \bmod m \f$</td>
     <td>the greatest common divisor, and the remainder in
         \f$ [ 0 , m ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \omega_n \f$</td>
     <td>the root of unity \f$ e^{-2 \pi i / n} \f$ of the discrete Fourier
         transform \f$ \hat a_k = \sum_{j<n} a_j \omega_n^{j k} \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ F_n \f$, \f$ C_n \f$, \f$ \binom{n}{k} \f$</td>
     <td>the numbers of Fibonacci (\f$ F_0 = F_1 = 1 \f$) and of Catalan,
         and the binomial coefficients</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathit{nnz} \f$</td>
     <td>the nonzero entries of a sparse matrix</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ g \f$, \f$ U \f$</td>
     <td>a generator of random words, and a real uniform in
         \f$ [ 0 , 1 ) \f$ or \f$ ( 0 , 1 ) \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_numeric_repr Representations and their costs

 The numbers and the matrices of the area are kept in several
 representations, each with the operations that it makes cheap; the table
 gives the space and the cost of the main ones (the products of the integers
 of any length depend on their length, see \ref theory_numeric_dc).

 <table>
 <caption>The representations of the area and the cost of their
          operations</caption>
 <tr><th>Representation</th><th>Space</th><th>Main operations</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>Bits</tt> of <tt>BinaryInteger.h</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td>
     <td>sum \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$, product \f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$ or
         \f$ \Theta( n^{\log_2 3} ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>BigInteger</tt></td><td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ digits of 64
     bits</td>
     <td>sum \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$, product from \f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$ to
         \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$, quotient, conversion and gcd
         \f$ O( n^2 ) \f$ by the code of the book, those of GMP on its
         backend</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>Rational</tt></td><td>two <tt>BigInteger</tt> in lowest
     terms</td>
     <td>each operation a few products and gcds of the terms</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>Matrix</tt>, <tt>DenseMatrix</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( m n ) \f$</td>
     <td>product \f$ \Theta( n^3 ) \f$, or \f$ O( n^{2.81} ) \f$ by
         Strassen</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>SparseMatrix</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( m + n + \mathit{nnz} ) \f$</td>
     <td>entry \f$ O( \text{its row} ) \f$, insertion and deletion of an
         entry \f$ O( \text{its row and column} ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>CsrMatrix</tt>, <tt>SellMatrix</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( m + \mathit{nnz} ) \f$, the padding for SELL</td>
     <td>product by a vector \f$ \Theta( m + \mathit{nnz} ) \f$, transpose
         \f$ \Theta( m + n + \mathit{nnz} ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>DynamicCsr</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( m + \mathit{nnz} ) \f$</td>
     <td>append of a row linear in its entries, removal of rows in one
         pass</td></tr>
 </table>

 The orthogonal lists of `SparseMatrix` are preferable when the pattern of
 the nonzeros changes, as in the elimination by pivots, whose fill-in enters
 the lists where it appears; the compressed rows are preferable when the
 pattern is fixed and the matrix is read, since a product by a vector then
 streams the values and the columns from memory in one pass. Among the dense
 representations, the vector of rows of `Matrix` follows the book, while
 `DenseMatrix` keeps the entries in one block, the layout by rows of the
 CBLAS, to which `gemm()` hands its products when there is one.

 \section theory_numeric_dc Divide et impera on the products

 The sum of two integers of \f$ n \f$ digits costs \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$, a
 bound that cannot be improved since every digit must be read; the product of
 the school costs \f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$, one partial product per digit of a
 factor. With \f$ a = a_1 \beta^h + a_0 \f$ and \f$ b = b_1 \beta^h + b_0 \f$,
 \f$ h = \lfloor n / 2 \rfloor \f$, the product is
 \f[
   a b = a_1 b_1 \beta^{2h} + ( a_1 b_0 + a_0 b_1 ) \beta^h + a_0 b_0 ,
   \tag{1}
 \f]
 four products of half size and \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ sums, whence
 \f$ T( n ) = 4 T( n / 2 ) + \Theta( n ) = \Theta( n^2 ) \f$, no gain
 (`multiplyFour()` of `Polynomial.h`, the Prodotto4() of section 2.6).

 <em>Lemma 1 (Karatsuba).</em> The middle term of (1) is
 \f[
   a_1 b_0 + a_0 b_1 = ( a_1 + a_0 ) ( b_1 + b_0 ) - a_1 b_1 - a_0 b_0 ,
   \tag{2}
 \f]
 so that three products of half size suffice, and
 \f$ T( n ) = 3 T( n / 2 ) + \Theta( n ) = \Theta( n^{\log_2 3} ) \f$, with
 \f$ \log_2 3 < 1.59 \f$.

 Indeed, (2) is an identity of the polynomials, and the recursion tree of
 \f$ T \f$ has \f$ 3^k \f$ calls of size \f$ n / 2^k \f$ at the level
 \f$ k \f$, whose total work \f$ ( 3 / 2 )^k n \f$ grows geometrically with
 \f$ k \f$; the leaves, \f$ 3^{\log_2 n} = n^{\log_2 3} \f$ of them,
 dominate. The same argument gives the bound of every split of this kind: a
 product cut in \f$ k \f$ pieces and rebuilt from \f$ r \f$ products of the
 pieces costs \f$ \Theta( n^{\log_k r} ) \f$ when \f$ r > k \f$. Hence
 Toom-3, five products of a third (the values of the polynomials at
 \f$ 0 , \pm 1 , 2 , \infty \f$ and an interpolation), costs
 \f$ \Theta( n^{\log_3 5} ) = O( n^{1.47} ) \f$, and Toom-4, seven of a
 quarter, \f$ \Theta( n^{\log_4 7} ) = O( n^{1.41} ) \f$, with constants that
 grow with \f$ k \f$. The code of the book in `BigInteger` passes from the
 school to Karatsuba at `kKaratsuba` digits and to a convolution of the
 digits at `kFFT`, round lengths that no processor tuned, while GMP, its
 backend, adds the methods of Toom and chooses its own thresholds; the
 convolution is computed by the Fourier transform in \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$
 operations
 (see \ref theory_numeric_fft).

 The matrices obey the same scheme. The product of two \f$ n \times n \f$
 matrices by the definition costs \f$ \Theta( n^3 ) \f$, and by blocks of
 \f$ n / 2 \f$ it is eight products of blocks, again \f$ \Theta( n^3 ) \f$;
 Strassen rebuilds it from seven products and eighteen sums of blocks
 (Winograd's variant, of `DenseMatrix.h`, from seven and fifteen), whence
 \f[
   T( n ) = 7 T( n / 2 ) + \Theta( n^2 ) = \Theta( n^{\log_2 7} ) ,
   \qquad \log_2 7 < 2.81 . \tag{3}
 \f]
 The price is the accuracy in floating point: the bound of the error grows
 with a power of \f$ n / n_0 \f$, \f$ n_0 \f$ the size at which the recursion
 stops, instead of with \f$ n \f$ (Higham), which is the reason why
 `multiplyStrassen()` stops early and leaves the blocks to `gemm()`.

 <table>
 <caption>The products of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Function</th><th>Method</th><th>Time</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>multiplyNaive()</tt>, <tt>multiplyBinarySchool()</tt></td>
     <td>the school</td><td>\f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>multiplyFour()</tt>, <tt>multiplyBinaryFour()</tt></td>
     <td>(1), four halves</td><td>\f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>multiplyKaratsuba()</tt></td><td>(2), three halves</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n^{\log_2 3} ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>BigInteger::operator*()</tt></td>
     <td>school, Karatsuba, the NTT, or GMP</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n \log n ) \f$ for the longest</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>multiplyNaive()</tt> and <tt>strassen()</tt> of
     <tt>Matrix.h</tt></td>
     <td>the definition, and (3)</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n^3 ) \f$, \f$ \Theta( n^{\log_2 7} ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>gemm()</tt>, <tt>multiplyStrassen()</tt></td>
     <td>the definition by blocks, or the CBLAS, and (3) down to
         <tt>gemm()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( m n k ) \f$, \f$ O( n^{\log_2 7} ) \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 The quotient, the square root and the conversions between the bases reduce
 to products. The quotient of Algorithm D of Knuth guesses each digit from the
 leading digits of the remainder and of the normalized divisor, a guess that
 exceeds the true digit by at most 2 (by 1 with three digits over two), and
 costs \f$ O( m n ) \f$ for a quotient of \f$ m \f$ digits, the method of the
 code of the book; Newton's method on the reciprocal,
 \f$ x \gets x + x ( 1 - b x ) \f$, doubles the correct digits at each step,
 so that the reciprocal, and with it the quotient, costs a constant number
 of products of the final length, \f$ O( M( n ) ) \f$, the steps on the
 shorter lengths summing to a geometric series, and the conversions that
 split the number by a power of the base near its square root cost
 \f$ O( M( n ) \log n ) \f$: these are the methods of GMP, while the code of
 the book converts by one short division per word, \f$ O( n^2 ) \f$. The gcd
 of Lehmer, which applies to the whole numbers the quotients found on their
 leading digits, spares all but one long operation per word of quotients
 (see the comments of `BigInteger.h`).

 \section theory_numeric_rec Recurrences and dynamic programming

 The numbers of Fibonacci, \f$ F_n = F_{n-1} + F_{n-2} \f$, computed by the
 recurrence as it is written, take a number of calls that satisfies the same
 recurrence, i.e., \f$ \Theta( \phi^n ) \f$ with
 \f$ \phi = ( 1 + \sqrt{5} ) / 2 \approx 1.6 \f$; the table of the values
 already computed, or the last two alone, take \f$ O( n ) \f$ (`fibonacci()`
 of `Combinatorics.h`). The same holds for the binomial coefficients:
 \f[
   \binom{n}{k} = \binom{n-1}{k-1} + \binom{n-1}{k} , \qquad
   \binom{n}{0} = \binom{n}{n} = 1 , \tag{4}
 \f]
 whose recursion makes \f$ 2 \binom{n}{k} - 1 \f$ calls (each leaf returns 1,
 and a tree with \f$ \ell \f$ leaves whose inner nodes have two children has
 \f$ 2 \ell - 1 \f$ nodes), against the \f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$ of the triangle
 of Tartaglia (`tartaglia()`), which fills (4) by rows.

 The numbers of Catalan count the binary trees: a tree of \f$ n \f$ nodes has
 a left subtree of \f$ k \f$ nodes and a right one of \f$ n - 1 - k \f$, and
 hence
 \f[
   C_0 = 1 , \qquad C_n = \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} C_k C_{n-1-k} , \tag{5}
 \f]
 filled in \f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$ operations by `catalan()`;
 \f$ C_n = \binom{2n}{n} / ( n + 1 ) \sim 4^n / ( n^{3/2} \sqrt{\pi} ) \f$,
 so that the parenthesizations of a product of \f$ n \f$ matrices, which are
 \f$ C_{n-1} \f$, cannot be enumerated. The chain of matrices
 (`matrixChain()`) has optimal substructure instead: the last product of an
 optimal order splits the chain at some \f$ k \f$, and both sides are then
 ordered optimally, whence the recurrence (section 13.3)
 \f[
   \mathit{DP}[ i ][ j ] = \min_{i \le k < j} \{ \mathit{DP}[ i ][ k ] +
   \mathit{DP}[ k + 1 ][ j ] + c_i c_{k+1} c_{j+1} \} , \tag{6}
 \f]
 \f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$ subproblems of \f$ O( n ) \f$ choices each, i.e.,
 \f$ O( n^3 ) \f$ time, filled by increasing length so that the two sides are
 known when a chain is solved.

 \section theory_numeric_nt Number theory

 <em>Lemma 2 (Euclid).</em> For \f$ m > 0 \f$,
 \f[
   \gcd( s , m ) = \gcd( m , s \bmod m ) , \tag{7}
 \f]
 and the algorithm that applies (7) until \f$ m = 0 \f$ makes
 \f$ O( \log m ) \f$ steps.

 Indeed, \f$ s \bmod m = s - \lfloor s / m \rfloor m \f$, so that the two pairs
 have the same common divisors. After two steps the pair \f$ ( s , m ) \f$
 has become \f$ ( r , m \bmod r ) \f$, \f$ r = s \bmod m \f$, and
 \f$ r < s / 2 \f$: if \f$ m \le s / 2 \f$, since \f$ r < m \f$, and
 otherwise since \f$ r = s - m \f$; hence the first number halves every two
 steps, and the worst case is that of two consecutive numbers of Fibonacci
 (Lamé).
 `extendedEuclid()` carries along the coefficients of Bezout: if
 \f$ m p' + ( s \bmod m ) q' = d \f$, then
 \f$ s q' + m ( p' - \lfloor s / m \rfloor q' ) = d \f$, and when
 \f$ d = 1 \f$ the coefficient of \f$ s \f$ is its inverse modulo \f$ m \f$
 (`modInverse()`). The binary gcd of Stein (`binaryGcd()`) replaces the
 divisions by shifts and subtractions, since
 \f$ \gcd( a , b ) = \gcd( \min( a , b ) , | a - b | ) \f$ and the difference
 of two odd numbers is even; each step halves the larger number at least,
 and the steps are at most \f$ \log_2 a + \log_2 b + 2 \f$.

 The power \f$ x^v \bmod m \f$ by repeated squaring (`powMod()`, exercise
 17.1) reads the bits of \f$ v \f$: with
 \f$ v = \sum_i v_i 2^i \f$, \f$ x^v = \prod_{i : v_i = 1} x^{2^i} \f$, the
 factors \f$ x^{2^i} \f$ are squares of one another, and the time is
 \f$ \lfloor \log_2 v \rfloor + 1 \f$ squarings and at most as many products
 modulo \f$ m \f$. The products modulo an odd \f$ m \f$ go by the form of
 Montgomery, \f$ \bar x = x R \bmod m \f$, \f$ R = 2^{64} \f$, in which the
 reduction of \f$ T = \bar a \bar b < m R \f$ is
 \f[
   \mathrm{REDC}( T ) = \frac{T - q m}{R} \equiv T R^{-1} \pmod{m} , \qquad
   q = T m^{-1} \bmod R , \tag{8}
 \f]
 exact since \f$ T - q m \equiv 0 \pmod{R} \f$, and in \f$ ( -m , m ) \f$, so
 that one addition of \f$ m \f$ at most ends it: two products and no
 division (`Montgomery64`).

 <em>Theorem 3 (Miller and Rabin).</em> Let \f$ n > 2 \f$ be odd and
 \f$ n - 1 = 2^v m \f$ with \f$ m \f$ odd. If \f$ n \f$ is prime, every base
 \f$ b \in [ 1 , n ) \f$ satisfies
 \f[
   b^m \equiv 1 \quad \text{or} \quad b^{2^i m} \equiv -1 \pmod{n}
   \text{ for some } 0 \le i < v , \tag{9}
 \f]
 and if \f$ n \f$ is composite at most a quarter of the bases satisfy (9).

 The first part holds since \f$ b^{n-1} \equiv 1 \f$ (Fermat), and modulo a
 prime the only square roots of 1 are \f$ \pm 1 \f$, so that the sequence
 \f$ b^m , b^{2m} , \ldots , b^{2^v m} \f$ either starts at 1 or reaches 1
 right after \f$ -1 \f$; the second is the theorem of Rabin, whose proof
 counts the bases in the group of the units. A base that violates (9) is a
 <em>witness</em> (`witness()`), and \f$ K \f$ random bases fail to find one
 for a composite \f$ n \f$ with probability at most \f$ 4^{-K} \f$
 (`millerRabin()`); the test errs only by saying prime. For \f$ n \f$ below
 \f$ 2^{64} \f$ a few fixed bases leave no composite unwitnessed, a fact
 checked by an exhaustive computation (Jaeschke, Sinclair), which makes
 `isPrime()` exact.

 RSA rests on Euler's theorem: with \f$ N = x y \f$, \f$ x \f$ and \f$ y \f$
 distinct primes, and \f$ p s \equiv 1 \pmod{( x - 1 ) ( y - 1 )} \f$, one has
 \f$ M^{p s} \equiv M \pmod{N} \f$ for every \f$ M < N \f$, since the
 congruence holds modulo \f$ x \f$ and modulo \f$ y \f$ (by Fermat if the
 prime does not divide \f$ M \f$, trivially if it does); hence the
 decryption \f$ C^s \f$ undoes the encryption \f$ M^p \f$ (section 17.3). The
 sieve of Eratosthenes (`sieve()`) removes the multiples of each prime
 \f$ p \le \sqrt{n} \f$ from \f$ p^2 \f$ on, the smaller ones being multiples
 of smaller primes already removed, in
 \f$ \sum_{p \le n} n / p = O( n \log \log n ) \f$ time by the theorem of
 Mertens.

 \section theory_numeric_fft The Fourier transform

 The discrete Fourier transform of \f$ a_0 , \ldots , a_{n-1} \f$ evaluates
 the polynomial \f$ a( x ) = \sum_j a_j x^j \f$ at the \f$ n \f$ roots of
 unity \f$ \omega_n^k \f$, and the inverse interpolates it, with
 \f$ \omega_n^{-1} \f$ and a factor \f$ 1 / n \f$, since
 \f$ \sum_k \omega_n^{j k} = 0 \f$ unless \f$ n \mid j \f$.

 <em>Lemma 4 (the convolution).</em> If \f$ c \f$ is the cyclic convolution
 of \f$ a \f$ and \f$ b \f$, \f$ c_k = \sum_{j} a_j b_{( k - j ) \bmod n} \f$,
 then
 \f[
   \hat c_k = \hat a_k \hat b_k \quad \forall k . \tag{10}
 \f]

 Indeed, \f$ c( x ) \equiv a( x ) b( x ) \f$ modulo \f$ x^n - 1 \f$, and each
 \f$ \omega_n^k \f$ is a root of \f$ x^n - 1 \f$. With \f$ n \f$ at least the
 number of coefficients of the product, the convolution is the product of the
 polynomials, and of the integers once the carries are propagated
 (`convolve()`, and the NTT of `BigInteger.h`, where the roots of unity are
 those of a prime field and (10) holds exactly).

 The transform is fast because it factors. With \f$ n = r m \f$, the
 index \f$ j = r i + j' \f$ of the input and \f$ k = k' + m q \f$ of the
 output, one has
 \f[
   \hat a_{k' + m q} = \sum_{j'<r} \omega_r^{j' q} \, \omega_n^{j' k'}
   \sum_{i<m} a_{r i + j'} \omega_m^{i k'} , \tag{11}
 \f]
 i.e., \f$ r \f$ transforms of length \f$ m \f$, \f$ n \f$ products by the
 twiddles \f$ \omega_n^{j' k'} \f$, and \f$ m \f$ transforms of length
 \f$ r \f$ (Cooley and Tukey). Applied recursively to
 \f$ n = 2^K \f$, (11) gives \f$ T( n ) = 2 T( n / 2 ) + \Theta( n ) =
 \Theta( n \log n ) \f$; `fft()` applies it as the four steps of Bailey, with
 \f$ r \f$ and \f$ m \f$ near \f$ \sqrt{n} \f$ so that each step works in the
 cache. A prime \f$ n \f$ has no factorization, and is reduced to a
 convolution: by the generator of the integers modulo \f$ n \f$, a cyclic one
 of \f$ n - 1 \f$ points (Rader), or by
 \f$ j k = ( j^2 + k^2 - ( k - j )^2 ) / 2 \f$, a linear one of any length at
 least \f$ 2 n - 1 \f$ (Bluestein); both are then computed by (10) on
 lengths that do factor, so that every \f$ n \f$ costs
 \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$. A transform of \f$ n \f$ real numbers is one complex
 transform of \f$ n / 2 \f$ points and a pass that separates the even and the
 odd items (`rfft()`), and the transforms of the cosines and of the sines are
 real transforms of the input reordered or extended (`dct()`, `dst()`).

 <table>
 <caption>The ways of a transform of \f$ n \f$ points</caption>
 <tr><th>Case</th><th>Method</th><th>Time</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ n \f$ small</td><td>a codelet, unrolled</td>
     <td>a fixed sequence of operations</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ n = r m \f$</td><td>the four steps (11), the rounds of
     Stockham for the long rows and columns</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n \log n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ n \f$ prime</td><td>Rader, a cyclic convolution of
     \f$ n - 1 \f$ points</td><td>\f$ O( n \log n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>a large prime factor</td><td>Bluestein, a convolution of
     \f$ M \ge 2 n - 1 \f$ points</td><td>\f$ O( n \log n ) \f$, a larger
     constant</td></tr>
 <tr><td>any \f$ n \f$, <tt>dft()</tt></td><td>the definition</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_numeric_random Random numbers and samplings

 A linear congruential generator \f$ X_{k+1} = ( a X_k + c ) \bmod m \f$
 (`LinearCongruential`) has a period at most \f$ m \f$, and exactly
 \f$ m \f$ if and only if \f$ c \f$ and \f$ m \f$ are coprime, \f$ a - 1 \f$
 is a multiple of every prime factor of \f$ m \f$, and of 4 if \f$ m \f$ is
 (Hull and Dobell); its pairs \f$ ( X_k , X_{k+1} ) \f$ lie on lines at the
 distance \f$ 1 / \nu_2 \f$, and the spectral test (`spectralTest2()`)
 measures \f$ \nu_2 \f$ by the reduction of a basis of the lattice of the
 vectors \f$ ( u , v ) \f$ with \f$ u + a v \equiv 0 \pmod{m} \f$; the same
 generator with \f$ a , c , m \f$ fixed at compile time
 (`LinearCongruentialT`, of which `MinStd` and `Mmix`) has `min()` and
 `max()` constant, as the distributions of the standard library require. The
 generators of `RandomGenerators.h` replace the linear map modulo \f$ m \f$
 with linear maps over \f$ \mathbb{F}_2 \f$ (xoshiro) or with the rounds of a
 bijection on a counter (Philox), followed by a nonlinear output. The
 empirical tests compare the counts \f$ Y_s \f$ of \f$ k \f$ categories of
 probabilities \f$ p_s \f$ with the expected ones,
 \f[
   V = \sum_s \frac{( Y_s - n p_s )^2}{n p_s} , \tag{12}
 \f]
 which for large \f$ n \f$ follows the \f$ \chi^2 \f$ distribution with
 \f$ k - 1 \f$ degrees of freedom (`chiSquare()`, `serialTest()`).

 <em>Lemma 5 (the shuffle).</em> The shuffle of Fisher and Yates, which for
 \f$ j = n , n - 1 , \ldots , 2 \f$ swaps the item \f$ j - 1 \f$ with one
 drawn uniformly among the first \f$ j \f$, gives each of the \f$ n! \f$
 permutations with probability \f$ 1 / n! \f$.

 Indeed, the draws are \f$ n ( n - 1 ) \cdots 2 = n! \f$ equally likely
 sequences, and they give distinct permutations, since the item put in the
 place \f$ j - 1 \f$ is never moved again. The uniform draw below \f$ j \f$
 is exact only if the generator gives it so: `uniformBelow()` takes the high
 word of \f$ x j \f$ and rejects the low words below \f$ 2^{64} \bmod j \f$,
 which leaves each result \f$ \lfloor 2^{64} / j \rfloor \f$ words, while a
 plain \f$ x \bmod j \f$ (as in `shuffle()` and `reservoirSampling()` of
 `RandomNumbers.h`) is off by at most \f$ j / 2^{64} \f$ for a generator of
 64 bits.

 <em>Lemma 6 (the reservoir).</em> If the item \f$ t \f$ of a stream,
 \f$ t > n \f$ (from 1), replaces a uniform item of a reservoir of \f$ n \f$
 with probability \f$ n / t \f$, then after \f$ N \f$ items each of them is
 in the reservoir with probability \f$ n / N \f$.

 By induction on \f$ N \f$: an item in the reservoir after \f$ N - 1 \f$
 items, with probability \f$ n / ( N - 1 ) \f$, survives the item \f$ N \f$
 with probability \f$ 1 - ( n / N ) ( 1 / n ) = ( N - 1 ) / N \f$, and the
 product is \f$ n / N \f$. Algorithm R (`reservoirSampling()`) draws a number
 per item, while Algorithm L of Li (`reservoirSampleL()`) draws how many items
 to skip, a geometric number, and only \f$ O( n ( 1 + \log( N / n ) ) ) \f$
 numbers in all; when \f$ N \f$ is known, the selection of each item with
 probability \f$ ( n - s ) / ( N - t ) \f$, \f$ s \f$ the items taken among
 the \f$ t \f$ seen, is uniform for the same reason (Algorithm S), and the
 method of Vitter draws its gaps (`sampleBySkips()`). The alias method
 (`AliasTable`) draws from a discrete distribution in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$: each of
 \f$ n \f$ cells of equal probability holds an item with a share
 \f$ p_i \le 1 \f$ and an alias for the rest, which the construction of Vose
 makes possible since the shares below 1 and above 1 always balance.

 \section theory_numeric_sparse Sparse matrices

 The product \f$ y = A x \f$ of a matrix in compressed sparse rows reads each
 nonzero once with the column that addresses \f$ x \f$, in
 \f$ \Theta( m + \mathit{nnz} ) \f$ time, and its \f$ 2 \, \mathit{nnz} \f$
 operations on \f$ 12 \, \mathit{nnz} \f$ bytes make it bound by the
 bandwidth of the memory rather than by the processor. The transpose is the
 matrix by columns, built by a counting sort on the columns: the counts give
 the starts of the rows of the transpose, and the nonzeros, scanned by row,
 land in each row of the transpose by increasing row, so that it comes out
 sorted in \f$ \Theta( m + n + \mathit{nnz} ) \f$ (`transpose()` of
 `CsrMatrix`); the same counting sort, by the major index, assembles the
 compressed form from triplets in any order (`SparseAssembly.h`). The format
 SELL-\f$ C \f$-\f$ \sigma \f$ (`SellMatrix`) stores the rows by chunks of
 \f$ C \f$, column by column, so that the vector lanes compute \f$ C \f$ rows
 at once; its cost is that of the padding,
 \f$ \sum_c C \max_{i \in c} r_i - \mathit{nnz} \f$, which the sort of the
 rows by length within windows of \f$ \sigma \f$ keeps small.

 The pivot step on the orthogonal lists (`pivot()` of `SparseMatrix`) is the
 elimination by exchange of Knuth: with the pivot \f$ a \f$ in the row
 \f$ r \f$ and the column \f$ c \f$, every entry \f$ d \f$ with a nonzero
 \f$ b \f$ in the pivot row and \f$ c' \f$ in the pivot column becomes
 \f$ d - b c' / a \f$, the pivot row is divided by \f$ a \f$ and the pivot
 column by \f$ - a \f$, and the pivot becomes \f$ 1 / a \f$; a pivot on each
 diagonal place in turn inverts a matrix whose pivots are not zero. Only the
 entries in the rows of the pivot column and in the columns of the pivot row
 change, and the new nonzeros (the fill-in) enter the lists where they
 appear.

 \section theory_numeric_backends The backends of GMP and of a CBLAS

 Where a mature library computes the same function, the headers of the area
 hand the operation to it, at every size, instead of keeping kernels of
 their own that each processor and each version of the library would call
 to be tuned again: `BigInteger` and `Rational` run on GMP when
 `ADS_WITH_GMP` is defined, and `gemm()` of `double` and `float` on a CBLAS
 (OpenBLAS, the reference CBLAS, Accelerate) when `ADS_WITH_BLAS` is; CMake
 and the makefile define each macro whenever the library is found, and
 leave it undefined otherwise. The dispatch is therefore the presence of the
 backend alone, with no threshold of length: the library chooses its own
 algorithms for the processor it was built for. Without the backend, as on
 a compiler for which none is installed, the headers run the algorithms of
 the book, written for clarity and portability with no assembly, no vector
 instructions and no constant measured on a processor: Algorithms A, S, M
 and D of Knuth, Karatsuba and the number theoretic transform for the long
 products, the conversions of section 4.4 and the gcd of Lehmer for the
 integers, and the product of the definition by blocks for the matrices;
 the same code is the reference that the tests compare the backend with.

 <em>Lemma 7 (the backends change no result).</em> For every operation of
 `BigInteger` and `Rational`, the result on the backend equals that of the
 code of ADS; for `gemm()`, both compute \f$ \alpha A B + \beta C \f$ by the
 classical product, each entry a sum of \f$ k \f$ products in some order,
 so that both satisfy
 \f[
   | \hat C - ( \alpha A B + \beta C ) | \le \gamma_{k + 2} \, ( | \alpha |
   | A | | B | + | \beta | | C | ) , \qquad \gamma_j = \frac{j u}{1 - j u} ,
   \tag{13}
 \f]
 with \f$ u \f$ the unit roundoff, though the two results may differ in the
 last bits.

 Indeed, the integers and the fractions of GMP are exact, as are those of
 ADS, and a fraction in lowest terms with a positive denominator is unique,
 so that the two routes return the same digits; the views of GMP on the
 digits of a `BigInteger` (`mpz_roinit_n()`) read them in place, both being
 sequences of words of 64 bits from the least significant, and the results
 are written into the digits of the result, or copied into them from a
 number of GMP, in \f$ O( n ) \f$ time, below the cost of the operation
 itself (the copy is the price of the representation).
 For the matrices, (13) is the bound of the classical product whatever the
 order of the sums (Higham, section 3.5), which differs between the loops of
 ADS and the kernels of the CBLAS; hence the tests compare the products on
 small integers, which both compute exactly, and the others within (13).

